WEBVTT

00:00:00.261 --> 00:00:03.086
Here's cos x, from zero to pi over

00:00:03.126 --> 00:00:05.932
two, with eight thin rectangles standing against it.

00:00:06.678 --> 00:00:07.710
Let's give the curve a meaning.

00:00:08.217 --> 00:00:09.451
A storm is easing off.

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:12.365
cos x is the rain rate, in centimetres

00:00:12.406 --> 00:00:14.855
per hour, x hours after it starts to

00:00:14.956 --> 00:00:15.136
ease.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.807
At the start, it's one centimetre an hour.

00:00:18.432 --> 00:00:20.317
By pi over two hours, about an hour

00:00:20.338 --> 00:00:21.942
and a half, the rain has stopped.

00:00:22.746 --> 00:00:24.900
So how much rain fell into the gauge?

00:00:25.665 --> 00:00:27.470
The rate keeps changing, so we can't just

00:00:27.592 --> 00:00:28.855
multiply rate by time.

00:00:29.579 --> 00:00:31.948
But adding up thin rectangles gives an estimate,

00:00:32.652 --> 00:00:34.178
and refining them makes it better.

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:37.020
Take one thin strip of time, with width

00:00:37.162 --> 00:00:37.805
delta x.

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:40.634
Delta x just means a small change in

00:00:40.856 --> 00:00:43.001
x, here a short stretch of time.

00:00:44.005 --> 00:00:46.231
Over that short time, the rate hardly changes.

00:00:46.815 --> 00:00:48.068
Call the rate f of x.

00:00:48.896 --> 00:00:50.842
So the rain that falls is about rate

00:00:50.923 --> 00:00:51.644
times time.

00:00:52.227 --> 00:00:54.513
On the graph, that's the rectangle's height times

00:00:54.614 --> 00:00:55.837
its width, its area.

00:00:56.601 --> 00:01:00.199
Centimetres per hour, times hours, gives centimetres.

00:01:01.024 --> 00:01:03.495
So each rectangle's area estimates the rain that

00:01:03.577 --> 00:01:04.842
fell during its strip.

00:01:05.285 --> 00:01:07.258
That's the change in the quantity over one

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:07.983
small step.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.243
Now add up all the strips.

00:01:10.909 --> 00:01:13.396
This sign, a capital sigma, means add them

00:01:13.618 --> 00:01:13.998
all up.

00:01:14.762 --> 00:01:16.707
The sum of f of x times delta

00:01:16.869 --> 00:01:17.891
x, from a to b,

00:01:18.394 --> 00:01:20.762
estimates the total change from a to b.

00:01:21.604 --> 00:01:23.647
With four strips, using the heights at their

00:01:23.688 --> 00:01:26.292
left ends, the total is about one point

00:01:26.414 --> 00:01:27.194
one eight centimetres.

00:01:28.117 --> 00:01:30.279
The rate falls across every strip, and each

00:01:30.381 --> 00:01:32.924
rectangle uses the rate at its start, so

00:01:33.085 --> 00:01:34.887
each one reaches above the curve.

00:01:35.532 --> 00:01:36.703
That's an overestimate.

00:01:37.492 --> 00:01:39.363
The height can come from any x in

00:01:39.384 --> 00:01:40.088
the strip's base.

00:01:40.793 --> 00:01:43.079
Use the right ends, and every rectangle sits

00:01:43.341 --> 00:01:43.921
under the curve.

00:01:44.484 --> 00:01:47.291
That gives about zero point seven nine, an

00:01:47.513 --> 00:01:48.133
underestimate.

00:01:48.837 --> 00:01:50.684
The true amount is somewhere in between.

00:01:51.538 --> 00:01:52.990
So make the strips thinner.

00:01:53.756 --> 00:01:56.381
Delta x is the whole interval, b minus

00:01:56.522 --> 00:01:58.906
a, split into n equal strips.

00:01:59.429 --> 00:02:02.437
So delta x equals b minus a, over

00:02:02.599 --> 00:02:02.638
n.

00:02:03.603 --> 00:02:04.748
Double n to eight,

00:02:05.311 --> 00:02:07.038
and the left sum drops to about one

00:02:07.099 --> 00:02:07.561
point one.

00:02:08.323 --> 00:02:10.907
Sixteen strips gives about one point zero five,

00:02:11.349 --> 00:02:13.773
and thirty-two gives about one point zero two.

00:02:14.597 --> 00:02:17.091
Meanwhile, the right sums climb towards the same

00:02:17.153 --> 00:02:17.413
place.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:18.466
Here's why.

00:02:19.378 --> 00:02:21.822
In a thinner strip, the rate changes less,

00:02:22.204 --> 00:02:24.227
so the gap between each rectangle and the

00:02:24.288 --> 00:02:25.189
curve shrinks.

00:02:26.013 --> 00:02:28.321
Both estimates close in on one centimetre.

00:02:28.824 --> 00:02:30.932
A later video shows why it's exactly one.

00:02:31.697 --> 00:02:33.891
Every estimate so far is an area.

00:02:34.656 --> 00:02:37.083
So the total change is the area of

00:02:37.124 --> 00:02:40.393
this region, between the curve and the x-axis,

00:02:40.955 --> 00:02:41.937
from a to b.

00:02:42.902 --> 00:02:45.072
That works when the curve stays on or

00:02:45.154 --> 00:02:46.841
above the axis, like this one.

00:02:47.868 --> 00:02:50.262
This curve is also continuous on the interval.

00:02:50.966 --> 00:02:52.769
Informally, that means you can draw it from

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:54.994
one end to the other without taking the

00:02:55.035 --> 00:02:55.916
pen off the paper.

00:02:56.539 --> 00:02:58.983
If the rain stopped dead halfway, the graph

00:02:59.104 --> 00:03:01.228
would jump, and the pen would have to

00:03:01.269 --> 00:03:01.509
lift.

00:03:02.373 --> 00:03:04.142
In this topic, the curves we add up

00:03:04.686 --> 00:03:05.630
are continuous.

00:03:06.636 --> 00:03:09.672
Rectangles have flat tops, but the curve slopes.

00:03:10.316 --> 00:03:12.564
So join the top points with straight lines

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:13.046
instead.

00:03:13.629 --> 00:03:16.180
Each strip becomes a trapezium, a shape with

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:17.446
two parallel sides.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.000
Its area is the average of the two

00:03:20.061 --> 00:03:21.388
heights, times the width.

00:03:22.133 --> 00:03:24.279
That's the average of the left rectangle and

00:03:24.321 --> 00:03:25.343
the right rectangle.

00:03:26.147 --> 00:03:28.718
With just four trapezia, the estimate is about

00:03:28.879 --> 00:03:30.586
zero point nine eight seven.

00:03:31.109 --> 00:03:33.356
That's closer than thirty-two rectangles.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.344
Here the curve bends downwards, so each straight

00:03:37.405 --> 00:03:40.089
top sits just under it, and the trapezia

00:03:40.551 --> 00:03:41.692
slightly underestimate.

00:03:42.302 --> 00:03:44.298
Let's compare the methods on one example.

00:03:44.921 --> 00:03:47.126
A trolley's speed is x squared metres per

00:03:47.167 --> 00:03:49.311
second, x seconds after it starts.

00:03:50.073 --> 00:03:52.077
Estimate how far it goes in the first

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:54.162
two seconds, using four strips.

00:03:55.005 --> 00:03:57.698
Speed times time is distance, so each strip's

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.185
area is a distance.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:02.756
Delta x is two minus zero, over four,

00:04:02.917 --> 00:04:03.738
which is a half.

00:04:04.481 --> 00:04:07.505
The heights at zero, a half, one, one

00:04:07.526 --> 00:04:09.708
and a half and two are zero, a

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:12.573
quarter, one, two and a quarter, and four.

00:04:13.274 --> 00:04:15.819
Left rectangles use the first four heights, which

00:04:15.859 --> 00:04:17.662
gives a half times three and a half,

00:04:18.083 --> 00:04:19.425
or one point seven five metres.

00:04:20.167 --> 00:04:22.532
Right rectangles use the last four, which gives

00:04:22.594 --> 00:04:23.575
three point seven five.

00:04:24.179 --> 00:04:26.754
The trapezia take the average, two point seven

00:04:26.795 --> 00:04:27.015
five.

00:04:27.822 --> 00:04:29.998
Now refine all three in a graphing app.

00:04:30.661 --> 00:04:32.925
At eight strips, the left rectangles give about

00:04:33.025 --> 00:04:34.047
two point one nine,

00:04:34.668 --> 00:04:36.952
the right about three point one nine, and

00:04:36.992 --> 00:04:39.075
the trapezia about two point six nine.

00:04:39.876 --> 00:04:42.323
At sixteen strips, the trapezia give about two

00:04:42.383 --> 00:04:43.526
point six seven two.

00:04:44.389 --> 00:04:46.443
All three settle near two point six six

00:04:46.483 --> 00:04:47.107
seven metres.

00:04:47.993 --> 00:04:49.018
Now look at the errors.

00:04:49.863 --> 00:04:52.798
Each time n doubles, the rectangle errors roughly

00:04:52.879 --> 00:04:53.261
halve.

00:04:53.724 --> 00:04:55.816
But the trapezium error drops to about a

00:04:55.918 --> 00:04:56.300
quarter.

00:04:57.005 --> 00:04:59.695
This curve bends upwards, so the trapezia sit

00:04:59.776 --> 00:05:02.285
just above it, and slightly overestimate.

00:05:02.968 --> 00:05:04.996
Here the trapezia improve fastest.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:07.271
Try it yourself in a graphing app: change

00:05:07.393 --> 00:05:08.057
n and compare.

00:05:08.649 --> 00:05:09.796
Here's one line to keep.

00:05:10.481 --> 00:05:12.690
Strip equals rate times width.

00:05:13.434 --> 00:05:14.942
Add the strips, then thin them.

00:05:15.927 --> 00:05:17.731
Each strip is a small piece of change,

00:05:18.234 --> 00:05:20.158
the sum estimates the whole change,

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.985
and thinner strips make the estimate better.

00:05:25.576 --> 00:05:25.939
Your turn.

00:05:26.825 --> 00:05:29.267
Water flows into a tank at eight minus

00:05:29.508 --> 00:05:31.870
x squared litres per minute, for the first

00:05:31.991 --> 00:05:32.470
two minutes.

00:05:33.312 --> 00:05:37.065
Use four rectangles with left-end heights to estimate

00:05:37.106 --> 00:05:37.587
the volume.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.959
Is it an overestimate or an underestimate?

00:05:41.684 --> 00:05:42.509
Pause here to answer.

00:05:53.318 --> 00:05:54.652
Delta x is a half.

00:05:54.995 --> 00:05:57.116
The left heights are eight, seven point seven

00:05:57.157 --> 00:06:00.258
five, seven, and five point seven five, which

00:06:00.419 --> 00:06:01.840
add to twenty-eight point five.

00:06:02.502 --> 00:06:04.969
Times the width, that's fourteen point two five

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:05.330
litres.

00:06:06.174 --> 00:06:08.841
The rate falls across every strip, so each

00:06:08.982 --> 00:06:11.127
left height is the biggest in its strip.

00:06:11.549 --> 00:06:12.516
That's an overestimate.

00:06:13.244 --> 00:06:14.639
Twenty-eight point five is wrong.

00:06:14.983 --> 00:06:16.411
It forgot to multiply by the width.

00:06:18.016 --> 00:06:18.479
Last one.

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:22.037
A cyclist's speed is recorded every two seconds.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:25.243
At zero, two and four seconds, it's zero,

00:06:25.444 --> 00:06:27.466
six and ten metres per second.

00:06:28.131 --> 00:06:30.828
Use two trapezia to estimate the distance travelled.

00:06:31.455 --> 00:06:32.327
Pause here to answer.

00:06:43.036 --> 00:06:45.212
Each trapezium is two seconds wide.

00:06:45.756 --> 00:06:47.561
The first is two times the average of

00:06:47.663 --> 00:06:49.668
zero and six, which is six metres.

00:06:50.251 --> 00:06:51.861
The second is two times the average of

00:06:51.942 --> 00:06:53.793
six and ten, which is sixteen.

00:06:54.297 --> 00:06:56.406
So the estimate is twenty-two metres.

00:06:57.336 --> 00:06:58.407
Twenty metres is wrong.

00:06:58.831 --> 00:07:01.425
It uses one wide trapezium, and skips the

00:07:01.527 --> 00:07:02.531
reading at two seconds.

00:07:04.349 --> 00:07:07.320
So thin strips turn a changing rate into

00:07:07.341 --> 00:07:09.047
an estimate of the total change.

00:07:09.711 --> 00:07:11.981
But every rate so far stayed positive.

00:07:12.786 --> 00:07:14.934
Here's a bath filling with the plug out.

00:07:15.517 --> 00:07:17.742
Its net flow is twelve minus two t

00:07:17.883 --> 00:07:19.667
litres per minute, over eight minutes.

00:07:20.651 --> 00:07:23.424
After six minutes, it's negative, and a rectangle

00:07:23.545 --> 00:07:24.590
hangs below the axis.

00:07:25.555 --> 00:07:27.524
What does a strip below the axis mean?

00:07:30.355 --> 00:07:31.238
Here are the key notes.

00:07:31.761 --> 00:07:33.529
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:34.031
to copy them.
